<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>



<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Kanawai

//kānāwai// — Hawaiian: law, rule, edict; the body of binding custom a people holds itself to.

''The binding pin.'' `kanawai` and `aka` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/aka`) sugar the SAME
`pranala family=transclusion`, split the same way aka's own ontology splits (PROPAGATION, against
`kahea`'s push-forward), and further split from EACH OTHER on one further axis — FORCE. `aka` pins
informatively: a citation, a cross-reference, a likeness the carrier names without binding itself to
it. `kanawai` pins BINDINGLY: a carrier declaring `<<~ kanawai "URI">>` declares the law it holds
itself to, not merely a work it cites.

''The paradigm case.'' A carrier's own normative verbs — `MUST`, `SHOULD`, `MAY` — bind under the
vocabulary `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119` fixes. Declaring `<<~ kanawai
"lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>` at a carrier's head states that binding directly: this carrier
holds itself to RFC 2119's modal force, not merely citing the document. That declaration renders as a
citation, the same way an `aka` pin to a reference target would — the FORCE differs, not the shape on
the page.

''Both pin; both render by the target.'' `kanawai` carries the same mechanics `aka` carries: pin a
`lar:` target, render by the target's own kind — a citation for a reference meme, a frozen image for a
content meme. A `kanawai` pinning a content meme embeds that content as a frozen image too, binding
the carrier to it exactly as the citation case binds the carrier to a cited work's force.

''English weave name: `law`.'' `kanawai` weaves in English as `law`. ''The kramdown shore maps `kanawai`
to `normative` and `aka` to `informative`'' — the two reference classes an RFC toolchain already
distinguishes — but the house itself never says "normative": `kanawai` carries the house's own word
for what a normative reference means, binding rather than informing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! Law (Kānāwai)

A kanawai MUST bind a source socket and a target URI.
A kanawai MUST carry `family:transclusion`.
A kanawai MUST leave the target unchanged.
A kanawai MUST stay inert, carrying no execution pulse.
A kanawai SHOULD carry `confidence` when the observation is uncertain.
A kanawai MUST stay acyclic — directly and transitively.
A kanawai declaration MUST read as the carrier binding itself to the target; a citation that carries
no such binding reads as `aka` instead.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/syntax>>

!! Syntax

```text
<<~ kanawai URI>>
<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>
```

`?` source resolves to the nearest enclosing socket.
Fragment `#slot-name` addresses a named ahu child directly.

Regex (canonical):
```
/<<~\s*kanawai\s+(\S+)\s*>>/
```
Groups: `[full, target-uri]`

Full pranala expansion:
```text
<<~ pranala from="?" -> to="URI" family="transclusion" lifecycle="instance" traversal="source-to-target" propagation="none" role="binding">>
```

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/schema>>

!! Schema (machine-readable)

Canonical TOML form, mirroring `aka`'s own schema with the `role` fixed to `binding`.

```toml
sigil          = "kanawai"
kind           = "edge-sugar"
layer          = "both"
default-family = "transclusion"
render-mode    = null
alias          = ["law"]

pattern = '<<~\s*kanawai\s+(\S+)\s*>>'

[captures]
to = 1

[family-contract]
role-recommended   = true
confidence-bounded = true
```

